		{
			"id": "112139758686673002",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://mastodon.social/@Meyerweb/112139758443365849",
			"author": {
				"name": "Eric A. Meyer",
				"url": "https://mastodon.social/@Meyerweb",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/305/000/855/309/234/original/8eb6102f7385d076.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-03-22T14:15:19.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> What kind of intrigues me is that on 4G throttling and up, it really does feel fast, which I wasn’t expecting given all the server calls.  HTTP/2 packing the calls into a single connection, I guess?</p><p>On Good 3G throttling and below, though, it really is deeply painful.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112139770739452914",
			"parentId": "112139758686673002",
			"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat/112139770739452914",
			"author": {
